  "headline": "Copper at highs and gold fading as yields near 5%: a US-led reflation, not a scare",
  "body": [
    "The board reads as a hot-growth reflation with risk appetite intact, priced in the US and paid for in dollars. Copper sits at 98% of its 52-week range, up 6.5% this week and 21% year-to-date, while gold has shed 6% over the month; the copper/gold ratio is up 10% on the month. Bitcoin confirms the appetite side, up 10% this week and 36% over three months and buying 26% more gold than a month ago — that is liquidity and risk-seeking, not fear.",
    "Rates fit the story with a hawkish edge. The 10-year at 4.96% is up 22 basis points over the month and 80 on the year, while the 30-year has added just 2 basis points, flattening the 30y–10y spread by 20 basis points. Gold falling as yields rise means real rates are doing the work: markets are pricing a firmer policy path against strong nominal growth, not a runaway long-run inflation premium.",
    "Two tensions. Textbook reflation brings a softer dollar; instead the index is up 1.6% on the month and 0.9% this week, so the growth impulse is US-specific. Brent's 10% weekly drop is the lone disinflationary voice, though at 59% higher year-to-date energy remains a live cost. Copper and rates carry more weight than one week of oil."
